    "title": "Ruth’s Chris Steak House dress code bans hats, gym wear and more",
    "text": "Diners heading to Ruth’s Chris Steak House may want to think twice before topping off their outfit with a baseball cap. The upscale steakhouse chain which operates three locations in San Antonio is reminding guests that its dining rooms follow a “business casual” dress code, including a rule asking patrons to remove hats when entering, according to People. Guests wearing ball caps may be directed to dine in the bar or lounge area rather than the main dining room, according to a notice on the restaurant’s website. ALSO READ: San Antonio firefighter David McCulley's Torcatha BBQ sauce coming in hot at Central Market stores The policy also outlines several clothing items that are not permitted in dining rooms, including gym wear, pool attire, tank tops, clothing with offensive graphics or language, revealing clothing, and exposed undergarments. The dress code reminder comes as the brand operates under new ownership. In 2023, Darden Restaurants, which also owns chains including Olive Garden and LongHorn Steakhouse, acquired Ruth’s Chris’ parent company in a deal valued at about $715 million, according to the Associated Press. The original Chris Steak House opened in New Orleans in 1927 and was later purchased by Ruth Fertel in 1965, when it was renamed Ruth’s Chris Steak House. The brand now operates locations around the world. Other restaurants in Darden’s portfolio have slightly different policies. For example, The Capital Grille encourages upscale attire but does not explicitly ban hats, though it discourages athletic wear and overly revealing clothing.",
